{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And you shall cook and eat.</b> This cooking is roasting by fire, from the language of (Genesis 40:10) הבשילו אשכלותיה, and likewise (2 Chronicles 35:12–13) ויבשלו הפסח." "<b>And you shall turn in the morning.</b> On the morning of the second day, which is a weekday, for it is impossible for him to travel from there on the festival which is a sacred day. Or one may say: ופנית בבקר והלכת לאהליך — 'and you shall turn in the morning and go to your tents' — roundabout, to Jerusalem, and from there he would go to his land. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted ופנית בבקר: from here we learn that there is an obligation of overnight stay on the night after the festival, in the city, in honor of the commandment. And so they interpreted: ופנית בבקר — all departures that you make from the Temple when you come to it shall be only in the morning. And the explanation of לאהליך, 'to your tents,' is even if he was the last [farthest] in the land of Israel, so that he would be able to reach his home and return a second time for the appearance of the festival of Shavuot. And because of this this verse needed to be written regarding the festival of Passover more than regarding the other festivals, for there is no free time to go to the Temple twice from Passover until Atzeret unless he departs from there on the morning of the festival of Matzot after he has eaten his Paschal offering there — and after he has stayed through the first festival day, he must lodge there, and in the morning, which is a weekday, he goes to his tents; and with this he will have enough time to reach his home and return for the festival of Shavuot. How so? Behold, our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the land of Israel is four hundred parsah by four hundred parsah, that is, the length of the land is four hundred parsah and its width likewise, and the Temple was in the middle of the land — so two hundred parsah was the journey for the farthest and last in the land of Israel. And in twenty days a person walks two hundred parsot, at a reckoning of ten parsot per day. And it is known that from Passover until Atzeret is forty-nine days; subtract eight of them — the days of the Sabbath and the last day of the festival — and forty days remain. And concerning this it says ופנית בבקר והלכת לאהליך — 'and you shall turn in the morning and go to your tents' — that is, within a period of twenty days; and in the twenty remaining he would need to return again to the Temple for the appearance of Shavuot."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:44.889183054Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}